{"parentUuid":"9c0cae5f-54aa-41a6-b289-4a800089f805","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NQU3GD9icmKRjLZ6NzLbpr","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Mosek 10 11 interior point LP solver 2025 license price academic\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Mosek - Academic Licenses\",\"url\":\"https://www.mosek.com/products/academic-licenses/\"},{\"title\":\"MOSEK Solver Engine License | solver\",\"url\":\"https://www.solver.com/mosek-solver-engine-software\"},{\"title\":\"Mosek ApS\",\"url\":\"https://www.mosek.com/\"},{\"title\":\"MOSEK - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MOSEK\"},{\"title\":\"Mosek - Personal Academic License\",\"url\":\"https://www.mosek.com/license/request/personal-academic/\"},{\"title\":\"MOSEK Solver: Download, Pricing & Documentation - AMPL\",\"url\":\"https://ampl.com/products/solvers/linear-solvers/mosek/\"},{\"title\":\"MOSEK – High-performance LP and QP Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://matpower.app/manual/matpower/mosekHighperformanceLPandQPSolvers.html\"},{\"title\":\"The Mosek Interior Point Optimizer for Linear Programming: An Implementation of the Homogeneous Algorithm | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4757-3216-0_8\"},{\"title\":\"Mosek - Institutional Academic License\",\"url\":\"https://www.mosek.com/license/request/institutional-academic/\"},{\"title\":\"Buy MOSEK License | Pricing, Quotes & Expert Support from AMPL\",\"url\":\"https://ampl.com/buymosek/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found:\n\n## MOSEK Version and Interior Point Solver\n\nThe latest stable release of MOSEK is version 11.1, which includes an interior-point optimizer, based on the so-called homogeneous model. MOSEK also supports linear, mixed-integer linear, quadratic, mixed-integer quadratic, quadratically constrained, conic and convex nonlinear mathematical optimization problems.\n\n## Academic Licensing\n\nAlthough MOSEK is a commercial package, at the time of this writing there is a free academic license available at https://www.mosek.com/products/academic-licenses/. The search results indicate that MOSEK offers both personal and institutional academic license options.\n\n## Pricing Information\n\nThe search results do not contain specific 2025 license pricing information for academic versions. While the results confirm that MOSEK is a commercial package with free academic licenses available, detailed pricing for commercial licenses is not included in these search results. You may need to contact MOSEK directly at their pricing page or request a quote for specific commercial licensing information.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"cc30c37f-da5f-4225-94ac-925963a4b3fb","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:30.875Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9c0cae5f-54aa-41a6-b289-4a800089f805","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"50033bb7-b139-4968-a964-7e5aa3bd9e13","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KeCJwjXoawPsbF8sM17NBA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PDLP cuPDLP cuPDLPx GPU LP solver 2025 2026 benchmark performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - MIT-Lu-Lab/cuPDLPx: A GPU-Accelerated First-Order LP Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/MIT-Lu-Lab/cuPDLPx\"},{\"title\":\"cuPDLPx: A Further Enhanced GPU-Based First-Order Solver for Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.14051\"},{\"title\":\"cuPDLPx: A Further Enhanced GPU-Based First-Order Solver for Linear Programming - ADS\",\"url\":\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025arXiv250714051L/abstract\"},{\"title\":\"cuPDLP.jl: A GPU Implementation of Restarted Primal-Dual ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.12180\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - COPT-Public/cuPDLP-C: Code for solving LP on GPU using first-order methods\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/COPT-Public/cuPDLP-C\"},{\"title\":\"Accelerate Large Linear Programming Problems with NVIDIA cuOpt | NVIDIA Technical Blog\",\"url\":\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/accelerate-large-linear-programming-problems-with-nvidia-cuopt/\"},{\"title\":\"cuPDLP+: A Further Enhanced GPU-Based First-Order Solver for Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.14051v1\"},{\"title\":\"[2507.14051] cuPDLPx: A Further Enhanced GPU-Based First-Order Solver for Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.14051\"},{\"title\":\"cuPDLP.jl: A GPU Implementation of Restarted Primal-Dual Hybrid Gradient for Linear Programming in Julia\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2311.12180v3\"},{\"title\":\"cuPDLPx: A Further Enhanced GPU-Based First-Order Solver for Linear Programming | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/393851768_cuPDLP_A_Further_Enhanced_GPU-Based_First-Order_Solver_for_Linear_Programming\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about PDLP, cuPDLP, and cuPDLPx GPU LP solvers and their benchmark performance:\n\n## Overview\n\ncuPDLPx is a further enhanced GPU-based first-order solver for linear programming, building on the recently developed restarted Halpern PDHG for LP and incorporating new techniques including a new restart criterion and a PID-controlled primal weight update.\n\n## Benchmark Performance\n\n**cuPDLPx Performance (2025):**\n\nAcross benchmark datasets, cuPDLPx achieves 2.5x-5x speedups on MIPLIB LP relaxations and 3x-6.8x on Mittelmann's benchmark set, with particularly strong improvements in high-accuracy and presolve-enabled settings.\n\nMore specifically, in various scenarios, cuPDLPx achieves a 2× to 5× speedup over its predecessor cuPDLP on hard instances (those requiring at least 10 seconds to solve).\n\n**Testing Environment:**\n\nThe experiments use an NVIDIA H100-SXM-80GB GPU, with CUDA 12.4. Numerical experiments are conducted on two widely used LP benchmark datasets: MIP Relaxations, which consists of 383 instances derived from root-node LP relaxations of MIPLIB 2017, and Mittelmann's LP benchmark dataset, which contains 49 LP instances.\n\n## Key Technical Features\n\ncuPDLPx incorporates a Halpern update scheme, an adaptive restart scheme, and a PID-controlled primal weight, resulting in substantial empirical improvements over its predecessor, cuPDLP, on standard LP benchmark suites.\n\nThe solver is publicly available at https://github.com/MIT-Lu-Lab/cuPDLPx.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"716e5483-d2b2-4f13-8dbf-545e4d90a76c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:30.878Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"50033bb7-b139-4968-a964-7e5aa3bd9e13","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"af9a6be2-8063-4bc5-a077-5e1c50f616f6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DRbw4tnR3MpGHiRNaNNL7D","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Clarabel.rs Rust interior point LP QP SOCP SDP solver 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - oxfordcontrol/Clarabel.rs: Clarabel.rs: Interior-point solver for convex conic optimisation problems in Rust. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/oxfordcontrol/Clarabel.rs\"},{\"title\":\"clarabel - Rust\",\"url\":\"https://docs.rs/clarabel/latest/clarabel/\"},{\"title\":\"University of Oxford Control Group · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/oxfordcontrol\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · oxfordcontrol/Clarabel.rs\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/oxfordcontrol/Clarabel.rs/releases\"},{\"title\":\"[ANN] Clarabel.jl: New convex optimization solver - Optimization (Mathematical) - Julia Programming Language\",\"url\":\"https://discourse.julialang.org/t/ann-clarabel-jl-new-convex-optimization-solver/83915\"},{\"title\":\"Clarabel: An interior-point solver for conic programs with quadratic objectives\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.12762v1\"},{\"title\":\"Clarabel.rs/Cargo.toml at main · oxfordcontrol/Clarabel.rs\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/oxfordcontrol/Clarabel.rs/blob/main/Cargo.toml\"},{\"title\":\"Interior Point Conic Optimization Solver • clarabel\",\"url\":\"https://oxfordcontrol.github.io/clarabel-r/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - goulart-paul/Clarabel-dev.rs: Clarabel.rs: Interior-point solver for convex conic optimisation problems in Rust. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/goulart-paul/Clarabel-dev.rs\"},{\"title\":\"Rust · Clarabel jl/rs\",\"url\":\"https://clarabel.org/stable/examples/rs/example_qp/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about Clarabel.rs:\n\n## Overview\n\nClarabel.rs is an interior-point solver for convex conic optimization problems in Rust.\n\n## Capabilities\n\nClarabel.rs solves linear programs (LPs), quadratic programs (QPs), second-order cone programs (SOCPs) and semidefinite programs (SDPs). It also solves problems with exponential, power cone and generalized power cone constraints.\n\n## Key Features\n\nUnlike interior point solvers based on the standard homogeneous self-dual embedding (HSDE), Clarabel.rs handles quadratic objectives without requiring any epigraphical reformulation of the objective, and can therefore be significantly faster than other HSDE-based solvers for problems with quadratic objective functions.\n\nInfeasible problems are detected using a homogeneous embedding technique.\n\n## Interfaces\n\nClarabel.rs comes with an optional Python interface. Additionally, there are implementations available in Julia, C/C++, and R through the Oxford Control Group.\n\n## Source\n\nThe project is maintained by the Oxford Control Group and is available on GitHub at oxfordcontrol/Clarabel.rs.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"1f657d2a-1033-42a6-ab83-a217df4999a1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:32.151Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"af9a6be2-8063-4bc5-a077-5e1c50f616f6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"510d4d73-9df0-474c-8710-f87f306fd6d3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C1LkGN1Ptk73oXLsibSNq2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"HiGHS solver 2025 interior point IPX LP MIP OR-Tools integration\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"HiGHS - High-performance parallel linear optimization software\",\"url\":\"https://highs.dev/\"},{\"title\":\"HiGHS optimization solver - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HiGHS_optimization_solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ERGO-Code/HiGHS: Linear optimization software · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ERGO-Code/HiGHS\"},{\"title\":\"HiGHS\",\"url\":\"https://www.gams.com/latest/docs/S_HIGHS.html\"},{\"title\":\"Solvers · HiGHS Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://ergo-code.github.io/HiGHS/dev/solvers/\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · ERGO-Code/HiGHS\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ERGO-Code/HiGHS/releases\"},{\"title\":\"About · HiGHS Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://ergo-code.github.io/HiGHS/stable/\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization solvers: the missing link for a fully open-\",\"url\":\"https://highs.dev/assets/HiGHS_funding_proposal.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"MAY 2025 HiGHS Newsletter 25.0\",\"url\":\"https://highs.dev/assets/HiGHS_Newsletter_25_0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"NEOS Server: HiGHS\",\"url\":\"https://neos-server.org/neos/solvers/lp:HiGHS/LP.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about HiGHS solver in 2025 regarding interior point methods, IPX, LP/MIP solving, and OR-Tools integration:\n\n## Interior Point Methods in HiGHS 2025\n\nVersion 1.12 of HiGHS introduced an interior point method implementation for solving LP problems, based on techniques described by Zanetti and Gondzio (2025), as an optional feature named HiPO that is dependent on the BLAS library.\n\nHiPO is based on a direct factorisation, with a parallel implementation following a factorisation-based regularised interior point method using the augmented system.\n\n## IPX Solver\n\nThe IPX solver can be forced to be used by setting the option solver to \"ipx\". When LPs are to be solved by an IPM solver, the HiPO solver is used (if available in the build) unless IPX has been specified explicitly.\n\n## LP and MIP Capabilities\n\nHiGHS is an optimization package for solving continuous and mixed-integer linear programming problems (LPs and MIPs) using simplex, interior-point, and branch-and-cut algorithms. HiGHS has a branch-and-cut solver for MIP problems.\n\n## OR-Tools Integration\n\nThe search results do not contain specific information about OR-Tools integration with HiGHS. You may want to consult the OR-Tools documentation or HiGHS GitHub repository for details on their current integration status.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a37e9c25-ca12-48ef-991c-d60931050224","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:32.909Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"510d4d73-9df0-474c-8710-f87f306fd6d3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"966778df-4b89-4011-9413-51c18d4f0705","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FXhC7pqi9N2nMMUZzGJwgW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"COPT solver Cardinal Optimizer 2025 LP benchmark vs Gurobi Mosek academic license\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"chenyitian-shanshu/SIRL-Gurobi · Hugging Face\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/chenyitian-shanshu/SIRL-Gurobi\"},{\"title\":\"The Year 2025 for GAMS Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://www.gams.com/blog/2026/01/the-year-2025-for-gams-solvers/\"},{\"title\":\"Latest Benchmark Results INFORMS Annual Conference 24-27 Oct 2021\",\"url\":\"https://plato.asu.edu/talks/informs2021.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"chenyitian-shanshu/SIRL-Gurobi32B · Hugging Face\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/chenyitian-shanshu/SIRL-Gurobi32B\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization Software | Marco Chiarandini, Pointers and Other Stuff\",\"url\":\"https://www.imada.sdu.dk/u/march/Blog/optimization/software/2023/02/12/optsoft.html\"},{\"title\":\"Decison Tree for Optimization Software\",\"url\":\"https://plato.asu.edu/bench.html\"},{\"title\":\"Do we know why open source MIP solvers are significantly slower than commercial ones? · ERGO-Code/HiGHS · Discussion #1683\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ERGO-Code/HiGHS/discussions/1683\"},{\"title\":\"An Overview of Math Programming Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://www.gams.com/blog/2022/09/an-overview-of-math-programming-solvers/\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Latest Benchmark Results INFORMS Annual Conference virtual 9 Nov 2020\",\"url\":\"https://plato.asu.edu/talks/informs2020.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding COPT Cardinal Optimizer, benchmarking information, and licensing:\n\n## COPT (Cardinal Optimizer) Overview\n\nCOPT (Cardinal Optimizer) is a mathematical optimization solver for large-scale optimization problems developed by Cardinal Operations, and it includes high-performance solvers for LP, MIP, NLP and so on.\n\n## Performance and Benchmarking\n\nCardinal Optimization has expanded into the area of nonlinear and nonconvex optimization, bringing a local optimizer for non-convex QCPs with COPT 7.2.5 and their first take on spatial branch-and-bound for (MI)QCP with version 8.\n\nRegarding comparative performance, for LP on Mittlemann's benchmarks the best open-source solver - HiGHS - is 20 times slower than the best commercial solver (COPT). Additionally, COPT solver achieves performance comparable to SIRL-Qwen2.5-32B-Gurobi across all optimization benchmarks.\n\n## Academic Licensing\n\nFor academic users, for academic users COPT is available for 365 days.\n\nRegarding Mosek's academic availability, Mosek is a commercial solver with academic license to solve linear and non linear convex optimization problems like LP, QP, SOCP and SDP problems, and mixed integer programming problems.\n\nThe search results show that the most successful solvers for MIPs are Gurobi, CPLEX, COPT Cardinal Solver, and FICO Xpress Optimization, which are all commercial and expensive (though, mostly free for academics).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"52d5320d-469f-493d-bd0b-3c9bc30adaa3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:33.036Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"966778df-4b89-4011-9413-51c18d4f0705","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"be2e155f-11cc-419f-b788-7fb71b0a8748","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PQviuGkZweSjpQzAWCmuGd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools MathOpt framework 2025 supported backends Gurobi HiGHS PDLP CPLEX\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/math_opt\"},{\"title\":\"The MathOpt Service | Operations Research API | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/service/math_opt/overview\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.math_opt.python.mathopt API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/math_opt/python/mathopt.html\"},{\"title\":\"Next major release will be 10.0 · google/or-tools · Discussion #3992\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/discussions/3992\"},{\"title\":\"Math Opt API\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/jPOfJuAvrpE\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/math_opt at main · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/tree/main/ortools/math_opt\"},{\"title\":\"Using Google OR-tools MathOpt Service to solve Optimization Problems on Google Cloud Platform · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/ebrahimpichka/1c676b185151292dcde54b5610e1eea8\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Next Release (10.0)\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/DKXja2YheCQ\"},{\"title\":\"Solve Optimization Problems on Google Cloud Platform using Google’s OR API and OR-tools MathOpt | Ebrahim Pichka\",\"url\":\"https://epichka.com/blog/2024/ortools-mathopt/\"},{\"title\":\"Google OR-Tools: ortools.math_opt.python.parameters.SolveParameters Class Reference\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/python/classortools_1_1math__opt_1_1python_1_1parameters_1_1SolveParameters.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about OR-Tools MathOpt framework's supported backends:\n\nUsers can switch between solvers like GLOP, PDLP, CP-SAT, SCIP, GLPK, Gurobi, and HiGHS, independently of the model's definition.\n\nAdditionally, supported solvers include GLOP, PDLP, CP-SAT, SCIP, GLPK, OSQP, and HiGHS for the MathOpt Service.\n\nRegarding CPLEX, while your query specifically mentioned it, the search results do not indicate that CPLEX is currently listed as a supported backend for OR-Tools MathOpt. The consistent list across the documentation shows support for GLOP, PDLP, CP-SAT, SCIP, GLPK, Gurobi, and HiGHS.\n\nThe documentation was last updated February 16, 2026, so this information reflects the current state of the framework in 2025.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7778c27c-2256-490c-963f-fb45948619fa","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:33.191Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"be2e155f-11cc-419f-b788-7fb71b0a8748","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0c1af292-cf48-47a2-8577-34e6c50c48ce","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018KxSPd876q1dfq63xTdMiD","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SCS splitting conic solver GPU 2025 LP performance benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - cvxgrp/scs: Splitting Conic Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cvxgrp/scs\"},{\"title\":\"SCS — SCS 3.2.11 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxgrp.org/scs/\"},{\"title\":\"scs: Splitting Conic Solver\",\"url\":\"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/scs/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"PDCS: A Primal-Dual Large-Scale Conic Programming Solver with GPU Enhancements\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/arxiv_May1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Solver Features -\",\"url\":\"https://www.cvxpy.org/tutorial/solvers/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"SCS (Splitting Conic Solver) - Neal Parikh\",\"url\":\"https://nparikh.org/publications/scs/\"},{\"title\":\"scs: Splitting Conic Solver version 3.2.7 from CRAN\",\"url\":\"https://rdrr.io/cran/scs/\"},{\"title\":\"scs · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/scs/\"},{\"title\":\"OPERATOR SPLITTING FOR A HOMOGENEOUS ...\",\"url\":\"https://bodono.github.io/publications/quad_scs.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"scs: SCS - Splitting Conic Solver in scs: Splitting Conic Solver\",\"url\":\"https://rdrr.io/cran/scs/man/scs.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about SCS and GPU performance benchmarks:\n\n## Overview of SCS\n\nSCS (splitting conic solver) is a numerical optimization package for solving large-scale convex cone problems. The current version is 3.2.11.\n\n## LP Performance and Benchmarks\n\nSCS is one of the most performant solvers as determined by the third-party QP solvers benchmark on the challenging Maros-Meszaros QP test suite, despite being a general quadratic conic solver and not specifically tailored for QPs.\n\nFor linear programming specifically, SCS v3.0 is the fastest solver on the NETLIB LP problem set. For feasible problems, SCS v3.0 was about 16× faster than OSQP and 20× faster than COSMO.\n\n## GPU Support\n\nSCS supports using the cuDSS solver that runs on a GPU, though it must be used with an SCS build that links cuDSS.\n\n## Related GPU-Enhanced Solvers\n\nThe search also found a 2025 paper on PDCS (Primal-Dual Large-Scale Conic Programming Solver with GPU Enhancements), which compares against SCS and commercial solvers like MOSEK and COPT.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9fae5a5e-461b-48c2-8cff-41834c44b9fe","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:33.643Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0c1af292-cf48-47a2-8577-34e6c50c48ce","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"8744bbab-0e61-4455-9e76-9b262b471cd2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SZsMsnwJKLSr6mFbkoqVKQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Gurobi 12 LP solver 2026 academic license benchmark Mittelmann\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"An Update on Gurobi’s Participation in the Mittelmann Benchmarks - Gurobi Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.gurobi.com/resources/update-on-gurobi-participation-in-mittelmann-benchmarks/\"},{\"title\":\"Decison Tree for Optimization Software\",\"url\":\"https://plato.asu.edu/bench.html\"},{\"title\":\"Gurobi\",\"url\":\"https://www.gurobi.com/pdfs/benchmarks.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - prioritizr/benchmark: Benchmark performance of exact algorithms solvers for conservation planning · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/prioritizr/benchmark\"},{\"title\":\"Visualizations of Mittelmann benchmarks | Interactive plots showing pairwise time differences for every instance and every solver\",\"url\":\"https://mattmilten.github.io/mittelmann-plots/\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking Optimization Software a (Hi)Story Hans D Mittelmann\",\"url\":\"https://plato.asu.edu/talks/china2019.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Benchmarks for Current Linear and Mixed Integer Optimization Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288179452_Benchmarks_for_Current_Linear_and_Mixed_Integer_Optimization_Solvers\"},{\"title\":\"Latest Benchmark Results - Hans Mittelmann\",\"url\":\"https://plato.asu.edu/talks/informs2021.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis of Commercial and Free and Open Source ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.tdp.cat/issues11/tdp.a114a12.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Mittelmann interior-point and simplex benchmarks 24 Jan 2021 · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/denis-bz/aa5cafdd28448ec3349c7542a3246791\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding Gurobi 12 LP solver benchmarks and the Mittelmann benchmarks:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Gurobi's Withdrawal from Mittelmann Benchmarks:**\n\nGurobi has made the decision to discontinue its participation in the Mittelmann benchmarks. More specifically, in August 2024 Gurobi decided to withdraw from the benchmarks as well and their results have been removed.\n\nWhile these benchmarks have historically served as a valuable tool for demonstrating advancements in computational mathematical programming, the sizes of many of the test sets no longer align with today's best practices for benchmarking.\n\n**Current Mittelmann Benchmarks:**\n\nThe Mittelmann benchmarks continue to be maintained at Arizona State University. Recent LP benchmarks include LPfeas Benchmark (find a PD feasible point; also for GPUs) (4-26-2026), LPopt Benchmark (find optimal basic solution) (4-29-2026), and Large Network-LP Benchmark (commercial vs free) (4-28-2026).\n\n**Academic License Information:**\n\nThe search results did not contain specific information about Gurobi 12's academic license features or 2026-specific benchmarks. To get more current information about Gurobi 12 specifically, you may want to visit Gurobi's official website or contact their academic licensing team directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c999d308-f399-4ac3-8f4a-b653147754d0","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:35.432Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8744bbab-0e61-4455-9e76-9b262b471cd2","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"24556e27-ac81-4f4b-bd94-33a743ca700d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T4e7ieY5WQYFpgU1vgPXwN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CPLEX 22 IBM LP solver 2025 academic free version\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Getting CPLEX Academic License and Upgrading to the Latest Version in 2026 - Cplex academic license and upgrading to the latest version\",\"url\":\"https://copyprogramming.com/howto/upgrade-cplex-studio-to-academic-version\"},{\"title\":\"IBM ILOG CPLEX Optimization Studio\",\"url\":\"https://www.ibm.com/products/ilog-cplex-optimization-studio\"},{\"title\":\"Download IBM ILOG CPLEX Optimization Studio | GARP\",\"url\":\"https://www.revealedpreferences.org/download.html\"},{\"title\":\"CPLEX Optimization Studio is free for students and academics!\",\"url\":\"https://community.ibm.com/community/user/ai-datascience/blogs/xavier-nodet1/2020/07/09/cplex-free-for-students\"},{\"title\":\"IBM CPLEX Solver: Download, Pricing & Documentation - AMPL\",\"url\":\"https://ampl.com/products/solvers/linear-solvers/cplex/\"},{\"title\":\"IBM CPLEX — AMPL Resources\",\"url\":\"https://dev.ampl.com/solvers/cplex/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"IBM ILOG Optimization Academic Initiative\",\"url\":\"https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/ibm-ilog-optimization-academic-initiative\"},{\"title\":\"IBM OPL CPLEX – Free Community Edition | Decision Management Community\",\"url\":\"https://dmcommunity.org/2025/11/13/ibm-opl-cplex-free-community-edition/\"},{\"title\":\"CPLEX free for academic use | Sebastian Pokutta's Blog\",\"url\":\"https://spokutta.wordpress.com/2010/02/22/cplex-free-for-academic-use/\"},{\"title\":\"NEOS Server: CPLEX\",\"url\":\"https://neos-server.org/neos/solvers/lp:CPLEX/LP.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about CPLEX 22, IBM's LP solver, and academic free access in 2025:\n\n## CPLEX 22 Version Information\n\nThe current stable version of CPLEX Optimization Studio as of January 2026 is version 22.1.2, released in December 2022 with continued updates and patches through 2025.\n\n## Academic Free Version\n\nThe academic edition, accessed through the IBM Academic Initiative, provides the complete, unrestricted CPLEX solver at no cost for students, faculty, researchers, and university staff. Unlike the community edition that restricts problems to 1,000 variables and 1,000 constraints, the academic license provides a production-grade optimization engine suitable for serious research and educational projects.\n\n## How to Access\n\nThe IBM Academic Initiative provides completely free, unrestricted access to CPLEX Optimization Studio for students, faculty, and researchers—simply register with your institutional email at https://www.ibm.com/academic/home. Registration is typically approved within 24-48 hours before downloading.\n\n## LP Solver Capabilities\n\nYou can Solve LP, MIP, MIQCP and SOCP models using advanced algorithms such as simplex and barrier methods, combined with branch-and-bound, cutting planes and pre-solve techniques.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4a65d50f-a0eb-4d0d-884c-dfaf460dc0a1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:36.437Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"24556e27-ac81-4f4b-bd94-33a743ca700d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"e174a443-f1ee-4f95-b6d2-1326b9427823","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a2bbf0cd35f724a3c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Amv2YhxrbzspVKeR8dvL2Q","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Mittelmann LP benchmark 2025 large scale interior point comparison\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Visualizations of Mittelmann benchmarks | Interactive plots showing pairwise time differences for every instance and every solver\",\"url\":\"https://mattmilten.github.io/mittelmann-plots/\"},{\"title\":\"Decison Tree for Optimization Software\",\"url\":\"https://plato.asu.edu/bench.html\"},{\"title\":\"Mittelmann interior-point and simplex benchmarks 24 Jan 2021 · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/denis-bz/aa5cafdd28448ec3349c7542a3246791\"},{\"title\":\"Hans D Mittelmann – Optimization Online\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/author/mittelmann/\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Benchmarks for Current Linear and Mixed Integer Optimization Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288179452_Benchmarks_for_Current_Linear_and_Mixed_Integer_Optimization_Solvers\"},{\"title\":\"Solve Linear Programs Using the GPU-Accelerated Barrier Method in NVIDIA cuOpt | NVIDIA Technical Blog\",\"url\":\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/solve-linear-programs-using-the-gpu-accelerated-barrier-method-in-nvidia-cuopt/\"},{\"title\":\"Linear Programming FAQ\",\"url\":\"https://lpsolve.sourceforge.net/5.0/LinearProgrammingFAQ.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Latest Progress in Optimization Software Hans D Mittelmann\",\"url\":\"https://plato.asu.edu/talks/informs2025.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Implementation of interior-point methods for LP based on Krylov subspace iterative solvers with inner-iteration preconditioning | Computational Optimization and Applications | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10589-019-00103-y\"},{\"title\":\"Expected LP Benchmark Insights\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/expected-lp-benchmark\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about the Mittelmann LP benchmark and large-scale interior point method comparisons:\n\n## Overview of Mittelmann Benchmarks\n\nHans Mittelmann of Arizona State University compiles benchmark results for optimization software, including tests of numerous simplex and interior-point codes for linear programming as well as branch-and-bound codes for linear integer programming; both commercial packages and downloadable free implementations are included.\n\n## Large-Scale Interior Point Performance (2025)\n\nRecent benchmark results show significant developments in interior-point method performance on large-scale problems. The linear programs in the test set are large, with about a dozen problems containing more than 1 million variables and constraints.\n\nWhen cuOpt barrier is combined with cuOpt PDLP in concurrent mode, it ranked first among open source solvers and second overall out of eleven solvers in public benchmarks (as of October 20, 2025). Additionally, internal benchmarks show cuOpt barrier delivering over 8x average speedup compared to a leading open source CPU solver and over 2x average speedup compared to a popular commercial CPU solver on a public test set of large linear programs.\n\n## Benchmark Collections\n\nLarge-scale solver benchmarks employ canonical collections such as Netlib, QAPLIB, Mittelmann, and Atomizer Basis Pursuit, spanning problems with sizes from tens of variables to over 400,000 unknowns and densities from sparse (<1%) to dense (>48%).\n\nYou can access the interactive visualization of Mittelmann benchmark results at https://mattmilten.github.io/mittelmann-plots/ and the main benchmark page at https://plato.asu.edu/bench.html.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e2ae51ad-00f3-4191-be4c-c1198e23d5e4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:38.410Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e174a443-f1ee-4f95-b6d2-1326b9427823","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
